WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
- We've got a great
show for you all today.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:02.820
I'm very stoked.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:04.650
Take a look at my audience, first of all.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:07.020
Everyone is holding a copy
of their favorite book.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.400
I love this show.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.750
That's because today we are continuing

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.763
my Stranger Reads initiative.

00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:15.536
♪ Stranger reads ♪

00:00:15.536 --> 00:00:16.500
(audience applauds and cheers)

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.090
The idea is very simple.

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:20.100
Sometimes your best book recommendations

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.110
can come from people outside your circle.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:25.350
Later in the show we'll all
exchange books, which I love

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.600
and I'm eager to hear
what you all brought.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.250
I hope I haven't read any of them.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.640
But first, today's show is
also full of amazing women

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.650
who have wildly successful careers

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.620
spanning many years and
including many Academy awards.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.187
Together they star in a new movie

00:00:39.187 --> 00:00:40.860
"Book Club: The Next Chapter",

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.330
it's in theaters tomorrow everybody.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.940
And our book club with
them is already in session,

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:46.389
right over here.

00:00:46.389 --> 00:00:49.020
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.530
Everybody say hello to
Jane Fonda, Candice Bergen,

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:53.657
Diane Keaton,

00:00:55.208 --> 00:00:57.039
and Mary Steenburgen.

00:00:57.039 --> 00:00:59.622
(band jamming)

00:01:00.631 --> 00:01:01.714
Hello ladies!

00:01:03.632 --> 00:01:05.910
I'm very excited to see you again.

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.040
- I'm so happy to see you again, Kelly.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.360
- And I'm excited to see you in person,

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.220
not on Zoom, Mary.
- I know, me too.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.070
- This is you in person and
welcome ladies to the show.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.740
I'm a big fan, obviously,
that's an understatement.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:18.990
- (chuckles) And we are fans of yours.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.460
- Oh, thank you, thank you
- Fans of yours, Kelly.

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.170
- Well, I'm gonna talk about
this 'cause it's in the movie.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.220
So let's dive right in.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.510
In the movie you read "The Alchemist"

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.620
which we all have a copy of.

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.960
I've read this, I actually love it.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.610
So in the story, the character though

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.800
learns what he was seeking

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.960
was actually back where
he started all along,

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:34.793
which I love that.

00:01:34.793 --> 00:01:36.720
So what does that mean to you, Mary?

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.720
- Well, I came from Arkansas
and the more places I go,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:45.360
the more I realize that
at the very core of me,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.140
I had it all as a child
with parents that loved me

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:50.850
and a community that loved me.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.210
And it's a great place to come from and,

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.340
but the book also talks about

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.740
never losing your sense of adventure

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.100
and that getting lost isn't
necessarily a bad thing in life.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.620
That it's all part of the adventure.

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:05.910
- I love getting lost sometimes.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.290
I'm literally about to take a vacation.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.150
I was like, "I don't wanna plan a thing."

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:10.320
I just like going somewhere

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.580
and just seeing where it takes you.

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:12.930
What do you all feel like from the book?

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.160
Did you like the book "The Alchemist"?

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:14.993
Did you like the story?

00:02:14.993 --> 00:02:16.380
The message?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.810
I love this quote, TS Elliot.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:19.807
It's a paraphrase.

00:02:19.807 --> 00:02:21.637
"We spend our life exploring

00:02:21.637 --> 00:02:23.887
"and at the end of our explorations,

00:02:23.887 --> 00:02:25.567
"we come back to where we started

00:02:25.567 --> 00:02:27.930
"and know it for the first time."

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:29.520
That's what you said.
- Yeah.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:30.353
Oh my God, I love that.

00:02:30.353 --> 00:02:32.125
Well, Candace and Diane, do you agree?

00:02:32.125 --> 00:02:33.953
- Yeah, absolutely.
- Yeah, of course.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:36.300
I mean, what else am I gonna do?

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:37.260
Duh.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.170
- Yeah, it's funny though,
I think when we're kids,

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:43.027
I'm from a small town and
I remember I was like,

00:02:43.027 --> 00:02:44.527
"I can't wait to get out.

00:02:44.527 --> 00:02:45.757
"I just wanna do so much.

00:02:45.757 --> 00:02:46.920
"I wanna see so much."

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.230
And it's funny now I live,

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:53.370
my main home is in a place
smaller than where I grew up.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:54.510
It's even smaller.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.970
'Cause you realize how precious that was

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.400
to have space and you know your neighbors

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.050
and you know everybody in the community.

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.300
It's funny how that comes full circle.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:04.200
Did you all experience that?

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.060
You're both from California, right?

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.250
- I'm from California and
I don't know about her.

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:10.260
- You're from Beverly
Hills I think, yeah, yeah.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:11.820
- I am from Beverly Hills, yes.

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:13.560
- I know, fancy.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:14.550
- She's from Beverly Hills.

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:15.510
- I know that's fancy.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:16.590
- I was from Highland Park.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.060
- You're from Highland?
- That's right.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.130
- Both fancy imperatively speaking.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:22.080
We'll talk more about
our favorite books later.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:25.050
But it's also Mother's Day this Sunday.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.973
So we wanna celebrate Mamas.

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:29.513
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:03:29.513 --> 00:03:31.110
Mary?

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:32.627
Mary, do your kids celebrate with you?

00:03:32.627 --> 00:03:34.200
Do you all do anything special?

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:36.600
- They do, but I'm a grandmother now,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.290
so part of what I'm celebrating
is my daughter Lily,

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.750
who's just an amazing mother herself.

00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:46.410
And yeah, it's like the whole,

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:49.530
any excuse for our whole
gang to just pile in.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:52.620
Dogs, kids, babies, everybody.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:53.760
It's wonderful.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:54.593
- That is wonderful.

00:03:54.593 --> 00:03:55.780
Especially as a grandma now, yeah.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.890
- Yeah, I love being a grandmother.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:01.350
It's so boring to say
but it is the greatest

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:02.312
thing in the world.
- I do too.

00:04:02.312 --> 00:04:03.480
- I know, it's special.

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.350
It's different.

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:08.790
- We text each other
grandmother photos all the time.

00:04:08.790 --> 00:04:10.380
- It must be so odd too.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.060
I have a six and eight year old,

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.800
but I can't, so obviously I can't imagine.

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:16.350
But to have your kids be mothers,

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:17.340
you know what I'm saying?

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:18.173
That's interesting.

00:04:18.173 --> 00:04:20.040
That dynamic like being
like, "Wait, you're mom too?"

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:20.873
That's just weird.

00:04:20.873 --> 00:04:21.847
I can't look at my eight year old and go,

00:04:21.847 --> 00:04:23.190
"I hope you do well."

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.950
Like, "I hope I did well enough."

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:26.783
What about you all?

00:04:26.783 --> 00:04:28.110
What do you do, Diane?
- Leave me out.

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:28.943
- You leave me out.

00:04:28.943 --> 00:04:29.776
You're like, "I don't wanna
celebrate Mother's Day."

00:04:29.776 --> 00:04:31.500
- I'm just gonna sit
here, I'm enjoying it.

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:32.973
- Okay, well I gave you wine.

00:04:32.973 --> 00:04:33.806
I like this and--

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:34.639
- And I'm glad I have it.

00:04:34.639 --> 00:04:36.007
- She's mildly inebriated.
- I like this.

00:04:36.007 --> 00:04:38.356
- I'm sorry I can't
share with you, everyone.

00:04:38.356 --> 00:04:39.660
(everyone laughs)

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.588
But I'm gonna drink up right now.

00:04:41.588 --> 00:04:43.080
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:46.020
- We're gonna celebrate
for you for Mother's Day.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.160
Let's take a quick break.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.050
Emma from our audience can help with that.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:49.883
- Hi!

00:04:49.883 --> 00:04:52.200
I'm so glad to be a part
of your book club today.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.170
The book I brought is "The
Storied Life of AJ Fikry"

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:56.700
by Gabrielle Zevin.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:59.610
It's all about how life
can take unexpected turns

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:01.710
and shape you into a brand new person.

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:03.060
I loved it. (chuckles)

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.980
It made me laugh and it made me cry

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:06.990
and I recommend it to everyone.

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:10.230
More with Kelly, Jane,
Candace, Mary and Diane

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:11.520
when we come back.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.220
- We're back with the cast of
"Book Club: The Next Chapter".

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.350
Jane Fonda, Candice Bergen, Diane Keaton

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:17.643
and Mary Steenburgen.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.740
So Jane, tell us what
happens in the movie.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:24.150
- In the "Book Club: The New Chapter"?

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:26.043
- The one that we're
- Yeah.

00:05:28.139 --> 00:05:29.760
How much do I give away?

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.010
We decide, I love this movie so much.

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:33.300
We decide--
- Not the ending.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.670
- That we're gonna go to Italy.

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:36.503
- I know.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:38.460
Just always a good decision.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.110
- Do I say why?

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.370
- Sure.
- Oh yeah, sure.

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:42.203
Okay.

00:05:43.470 --> 00:05:45.507
Me, what's my character's name?

00:05:45.507 --> 00:05:46.403
(everyone laughs)

00:05:46.403 --> 00:05:48.065
- Vivian.
- Vivian.

00:05:48.065 --> 00:05:49.380
- Do I have to do this?
- So far my favorite name.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.260
- She's gonna get married
and believe it or not,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:55.380
and so we decide to do a
bachelorette party in Italy.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.290
And so it's our adventures in--

00:05:58.290 --> 00:05:59.760
- Italy.
- In Italy.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:04.290
And, eventually our lovers, our
husbands or whoever they are

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:08.579
show up and and it's like,
"Why do we need them?"

00:06:08.579 --> 00:06:11.250
- Yeah.
- 'Cause a lot there is us.

00:06:11.250 --> 00:06:14.940
- I got to be with a Andy
Garcia for few minutes.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:16.620
So, it was really fun for me.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.641
- Yes.
- He had to kiss me too.

00:06:18.641 --> 00:06:20.226
(everyone laughs)
(audience cheers)

00:06:20.226 --> 00:06:21.397
- She hated it.

00:06:21.397 --> 00:06:23.520
She hated every second.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:25.269
- I can't wait to meet all the men here.

00:06:25.269 --> 00:06:26.970
(everyone laughs)

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.641
- Oh my God, I love you
with a little wine, okay.

00:06:28.641 --> 00:06:29.912
- Oh yeah.
(everyone laughs)

00:06:29.912 --> 00:06:30.971
- [Kelly] This is soft?

00:06:30.971 --> 00:06:31.804
Okay.

00:06:31.804 --> 00:06:33.270
Mary, did you get to do anything

00:06:33.270 --> 00:06:34.440
besides work when you were in Italy?

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:35.700
Did you all get to do anything fun?

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.610
- Well, I took Ted dancing,
so that was fun and--

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:39.540
- Never heard of him.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:43.440
- And our dog Arthur, our 17
and a half year old puppy dog

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:48.440
and he was, and Ted kind of
was everybody's plus one.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:52.650
He took us all out to dinner and I mean,

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:54.750
it's the food is just,

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:58.740
and she made me get
started on Aperol Spritzes,

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.840
which I don't think I'd
ever had in my life.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.470
And then I had to have one
every night breaking my rule

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.260
of don't drink when you're
working Mary Steenburgen,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.210
that was out the window and--

00:07:09.210 --> 00:07:10.800
- Doesn't count when
you're in Italy, Mary.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:11.670
- It didn't count.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.980
And I had so much fun
and I have no regrets.

00:07:13.980 --> 00:07:14.813
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:07:14.813 --> 00:07:15.900
And you just say, "I'm going method."

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:17.790
That's what you say in that environment.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:18.623
- I should have thought of that but.

00:07:18.623 --> 00:07:19.620
- This is for my character art.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.570
- Yeah, exactly.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.430
- So the first time everyone
here worked together

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:24.840
was the first "Book Club".

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:25.770
So Candace,

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:27.930
but you and Jane have known
each other for a while, right?

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:30.570
- Hundreds of years.
- Hundreds of years.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:32.430
- Since I was 17.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:34.440
- That's right.
- Are you serious?

00:07:34.440 --> 00:07:36.930
- My boyfriend had a crush
on her, thank you very much.

00:07:36.930 --> 00:07:40.780
And I guess he knew where she lived

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:44.046
and so he took me to her
house and we rang the doorbell

00:07:44.046 --> 00:07:46.290
and somebody opened it

00:07:46.290 --> 00:07:49.710
and she at 17 was on the top of a ladder.

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:51.577
And I just remember
looking up and thinking,

00:07:51.577 --> 00:07:55.027
"Oh, I never wanna be around her again.

00:07:55.027 --> 00:07:56.737
"This is the most beautiful human being

00:07:56.737 --> 00:07:58.140
"I have ever seen in my life."

00:07:58.140 --> 00:07:59.670
And plus she's brilliant

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.770
and she's written many
books, speaking of books.

00:08:01.770 --> 00:08:03.390
So we go back, we go back.

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:04.230
- We do.
- Yeah.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:05.310
- I love that kind of friendship

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:06.300
and then you get to work together.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:07.133
That's cool.
- Yeah.

00:08:07.133 --> 00:08:08.280
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:10.500
Well, Mary, we've talked
about your music in the past

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:12.690
which obviously you're on
the right show for that.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:14.760
So you wrote a song for this movie

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.563
that everybody sings at the end, right?

00:08:16.563 --> 00:08:19.800
- That was my love song to them.

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:21.540
I truly love them.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:24.547
And so in trying to write
a song, I just thought,

00:08:24.547 --> 00:08:26.430
"It's gotta be a love song to them."

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:28.080
And it's called "Anywhere With You".

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.840
And I wrote it with Caitlin
Smith and Troy Verges

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:32.550
who you know of from Nashville.

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:33.383
- Yeah.

00:08:33.383 --> 00:08:36.930
- And before we didn't have
time to get you to sing it.

00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:37.920
So we sang it.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:40.170
The four of us sing it to each other.

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:42.390
- I think that's cooler
that you all did it.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:43.560
That's the coolest thing ever.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:44.393
How did you all feel about singing it?

00:08:44.393 --> 00:08:46.800
Was that good?
- It was wonderful, yeah.

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:47.640
We love the song.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:48.473
- I love you.

00:08:48.473 --> 00:08:49.312
I rocked it.
- Yeah.

00:08:49.312 --> 00:08:50.580
(Kelly and Jane laugh)

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:51.930
I killed it.

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.060
So Diane, were you surprised
that she was a songwriter?

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.500
Did you know that about Mary?

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:58.110
- I knew that she was,
yeah, musically inclined

00:08:58.110 --> 00:08:59.850
and did things like that.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:02.490
But this is beautiful, this song.

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:03.990
- I just, I know--
- Did you hear it?

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:06.750
- I did and I knew of more
musical skills by the way,

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:08.670
'cause I don't know if
we're ready to pull this up

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:11.820
but I wanna pull it up
'cause it's my favorite thing

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:15.057
about this interview is you
dancing on TikTok, you're a hit.

00:09:15.057 --> 00:09:16.530
- Oh, oh.
- I don't know.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:18.492
I don't know if you--
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:09:18.492 --> 00:09:19.943
- I am not a dancer.
- I saw that yesterday.

00:09:19.943 --> 00:09:22.290
- Because I'm an idiot.
- Are you serious?

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:23.123
No, I am not kidding.

00:09:23.123 --> 00:09:24.780
- I was just in my stupid--

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:25.761
- [Kelly] I'm not even being funny.

00:09:25.761 --> 00:09:27.916
- Was it to Miley, "Flowers"?

00:09:27.916 --> 00:09:29.283
I know it, I saw it.
- Let's see it.

00:09:29.283 --> 00:09:30.297
- It's so good.
- Let's show it.

00:09:30.297 --> 00:09:31.631
- What?
- Okay, here we go.

00:09:31.631 --> 00:09:35.545
♪ I can buy myself flowers ♪

00:09:35.545 --> 00:09:39.462
♪ Write my name in the sand ♪

00:09:39.462 --> 00:09:43.426
♪ Talk to myself for hours ♪

00:09:43.426 --> 00:09:47.771
♪ Say things you don't understand ♪

00:09:47.771 --> 00:09:50.519
♪ I can take myself dancing ♪

00:09:50.519 --> 00:09:55.090
Get it!
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:09:55.090 --> 00:09:58.053
I love it, you're like not about it.

00:09:58.053 --> 00:10:01.650
It's literally like, I didn't
think I could love you more.

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:03.144
And I watched this and I was like,

00:10:03.144 --> 00:10:06.840
the best part is you're like,
"This looks like a good spot."

00:10:06.840 --> 00:10:07.830
And then you go for it

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:10.050
and then you're like, "Maybe this spot."

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:11.760
And it's (chuckles).

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.260
That's literally me at my house.

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:14.400
I love dancing.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.080
I own it all the time on this show.

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.690
When I'm home alone, I dance in my house

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:20.910
and I think, even in front of
my kids, they think I'm nuts.

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:22.290
But I love dancing.

00:10:22.290 --> 00:10:24.060
It looks like you feel
good when you do it.

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:26.310
- I had a fun time, but I'm a moron.

00:10:26.310 --> 00:10:28.200
- No you're not.
- I am.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:29.550
- No, I don't call that being a moron,

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:31.110
I call that feeling free

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.120
and vulnerable and awesome.
- It was fun, yeah.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:34.410
- Yeah. I love that video.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:36.484
I was like, "We're showing it."

00:10:36.484 --> 00:10:37.317
So--

00:10:37.317 --> 00:10:40.050
- And her whole Instagram
feed, it takes hours.

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:42.240
It's huge feed

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:45.540
and all of it is, she has
one part that's doors.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.070
It's just doors.
- All about doors.

00:10:47.070 --> 00:10:48.627
You don't wanna miss out on the doors.

00:10:48.627 --> 00:10:50.173
(audience laughs)

00:10:50.173 --> 00:10:51.330
- I'm not kidding.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:52.260
You are blowing.

00:10:52.260 --> 00:10:53.520
I don't even know if you know this

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.410
but you have blown up on TikTok.

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:56.492
Like literally--
- I have?

00:10:56.492 --> 00:10:57.755
- Yes.
- I have no idea.

00:10:57.755 --> 00:10:59.236
And what is TikTok?

00:10:59.236 --> 00:11:00.720
I don't even know what it is.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:03.870
- I know I get it, but
you are, it's insane.

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:04.980
Everybody's like, "Did you see the video?"

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:05.947
And I'm like, "I did.

00:11:05.947 --> 00:11:06.927
"It's incredible."

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:09.690
I think it's, and first
of all, I love doors.

00:11:09.690 --> 00:11:10.710
What are they like different doors

00:11:10.710 --> 00:11:12.660
all over the world?
- Yeah. I like the doors, yes.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:14.220
- Oh, I thought you meant like actual--

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:15.120
- No doors, yeah.

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.770
- I used to do that in Spain.

00:11:16.770 --> 00:11:18.540
When I walked around in
Spain I used to take pictures

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:19.373
'cause all the doors architecture--

00:11:19.373 --> 00:11:20.298
- Because they're amazing.

00:11:20.298 --> 00:11:21.270
Yeah, that's right.

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:22.800
- Yeah, and I'm obsessed with them.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:23.633
- I'm right, see.

00:11:23.633 --> 00:11:25.710
- We have so much in common and wine.

00:11:25.710 --> 00:11:27.817
So the official title of the new movie is

00:11:27.817 --> 00:11:29.496
"Book club: The Next Chapter".

00:11:29.496 --> 00:11:31.920
So what's a chapter age in your life

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.020
that you're kind of glad that's over?

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.250
Mine's my twenties.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:36.083
- Me too.

00:11:36.083 --> 00:11:36.916
My youth.

00:11:36.916 --> 00:11:38.400
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:39.600
- It's wasted on youth, life--

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:41.220
- Really hard to be young.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:42.840
- Yeah, my twenties were really difficult.

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:43.790
What about you all?

00:11:44.670 --> 00:11:48.780
- I just remember on the oldest
I've ever felt in my life

00:11:48.780 --> 00:11:51.060
was my 30th birthday.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:54.030
And it was just because I was sad.

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:57.453
There was a lot of stuff
not great in my life.

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:02.280
And here I was, I just
had my 70th birthday

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:05.040
and my 70th birthday was spent

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.110
recording that song with these guys

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.479
at Harvey Mason Jr. Studio.

00:12:09.479 --> 00:12:10.826
- I love Harvey Mason.

00:12:10.826 --> 00:12:12.600
- I love Harvey Mason.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:15.000
And so that's what we did.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:18.967
And if you had told me at
30, "On your 70th birthday

00:12:18.967 --> 00:12:22.537
"you'll be recording a song that you wrote

00:12:22.537 --> 00:12:25.807
"with three legends and you're all singing

00:12:25.807 --> 00:12:28.177
"and they're your true friends in life.

00:12:28.177 --> 00:12:29.520
"And I would've--"

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:30.720
- And they're your words.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:33.493
- And they're my words along
with my two co-writers.

00:12:33.493 --> 00:12:36.450
I would've been blown away, but I didn't.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:38.340
So the thing is

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:39.930
you don't know what
your journey's gonna be.

00:12:39.930 --> 00:12:41.880
You gotta say yes to stuff

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:44.760
that you might never
dream you would ever do.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:45.593
Don't give up.
(audience applauds)

00:12:45.593 --> 00:12:46.638
- Great.
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:12:46.638 --> 00:12:47.670
- Diane, what about you?

00:12:47.670 --> 00:12:49.087
What was maybe in your
life that you were like,

00:12:49.087 --> 00:12:51.387
"That was really tough,
I'm glad that's over."

00:12:52.477 --> 00:12:54.540
- "Really tough, I'm glad it's over,"

00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:57.682
every day I feel that way.
(everyone laughs)

00:12:57.682 --> 00:13:00.270
Kind of sad, not today obviously.

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:02.095
Except I have this.

00:13:02.095 --> 00:13:02.928
And I'm not sharing it with anyone.

00:13:02.928 --> 00:13:04.553
- Any day that didn't have that.

00:13:04.553 --> 00:13:06.003
Candace what about you?

00:13:07.502 --> 00:13:11.521
(Candace clears throat)

00:13:11.521 --> 00:13:15.330
I was glad to get the
young years out of the way.

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:20.330
And I think now is a
good time and I'm 76, so

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:24.600
I'm hoping I have a little
more of a clip to go.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:27.270
- Yeah. Isn't it interesting
though that you think, like

00:13:27.270 --> 00:13:28.242
I remember in my twenties I was like,

00:13:28.242 --> 00:13:29.527
"Okay, I gotta do everything.

00:13:29.527 --> 00:13:31.140
"I gotta do everything, fit it all in."

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:33.090
And this is the time and it's like,

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:34.800
honestly now that I'm older,

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:37.270
I'm 40 and I'm like, I'm having more fun.

00:13:37.270 --> 00:13:39.840
I'm smarter about things
like wiser about my schedule.

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:41.700
I'm just, you know, you can say no

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:42.533
you know what I'm saying?

00:13:42.533 --> 00:13:44.760
And things are still
gonna be fine your career.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.760
Like, it's interesting,
I feel like with age

00:13:47.760 --> 00:13:51.900
just comes wisdom for some not everyone,

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:54.570
but it's interesting though
that the older you get

00:13:54.570 --> 00:13:56.029
you're saying like, "I'm 76 now,"

00:13:56.029 --> 00:13:58.560
and you're still like
you're in this huge movie,

00:13:58.560 --> 00:13:59.730
you're with friends doing it

00:13:59.730 --> 00:14:02.430
and it's like things are still
hitting on all cylinders.

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:03.263
And I think that's incredible.

00:14:03.263 --> 00:14:04.096
- It's great.

00:14:04.096 --> 00:14:05.490
I'm stunned and I'm thrilled.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:06.405
- Yeah, me too.

00:14:06.405 --> 00:14:07.350
That's what I was saying, like in 40

00:14:07.350 --> 00:14:08.280
who would've thought I had a talk show?

00:14:08.280 --> 00:14:10.500
Who would've thought my life
would've taken this road?

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:12.510
I think it's interesting to say yes

00:14:12.510 --> 00:14:14.160
to things that you
would've never said yes to

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.720
and then you have no idea
where that will take you.

00:14:15.720 --> 00:14:16.980
I love that.

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:19.200
Well, Mary, you've said
that "The Book Club" movies

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:21.480
help women avoid experiencing
the diminishment plan.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:23.279
So what exactly is that you think?

00:14:23.279 --> 00:14:26.700
- I think there's this sort
of collective agreement

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:29.160
as we get older that's very subconscious.

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:33.420
That we're just gonna, we
agree to diminish ourselves.

00:14:33.420 --> 00:14:36.420
And I just don't think
you have to do that.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:38.070
And I know there's hard things

00:14:38.070 --> 00:14:40.800
and part of it's how well
you take care of your body

00:14:40.800 --> 00:14:44.899
and part of it's what
you're dealing with in life.

00:14:44.899 --> 00:14:47.850
But I also feel like you can fight it.

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:50.946
And Jane Fonda, for example,

00:14:50.946 --> 00:14:55.230
would be an example of
someone who just doesn't,

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:58.230
she refuses to be less
vibrant as she ages,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.960
actually is true of them all.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:02.730
And--
- And you too.

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:03.563
- And me too.

00:15:03.563 --> 00:15:04.918
I do try, I do care about that.

00:15:04.918 --> 00:15:06.060
- There's a point that
people get more vibrant.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.550
- I think that's the whole idea.

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:10.316
You're still alive.

00:15:10.316 --> 00:15:13.830
I was saying one day
that my only regrets are

00:15:13.830 --> 00:15:16.410
that my dad never met Ted.

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:19.080
I can't do anything about
that because my dad's gone.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:20.850
But my other regret was that

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:22.860
I didn't speak a foreign language.

00:15:22.860 --> 00:15:26.437
And then I suddenly realized,
"Well, you're still alive.

00:15:26.437 --> 00:15:27.690
"You can actually."

00:15:27.690 --> 00:15:30.900
So I've been studying French
for two years, which I'm,

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:34.410
they all speak languages like up the wazu

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:37.763
and I'm not very fast at
it, but I'm still doing it.

00:15:37.763 --> 00:15:39.330
I do it every day.

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:40.163
- Because you can.

00:15:40.163 --> 00:15:42.360
'Cause I think ageism is a real
thing and people think that

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:43.507
once you pass a certain thing,

00:15:43.507 --> 00:15:44.407
"Oh well that's your vocation.

00:15:44.407 --> 00:15:45.907
"You've already chosen what
you're gonna do for your life.

00:15:45.907 --> 00:15:46.950
"You've already chosen that."

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:47.970
That's not true at all.

00:15:47.970 --> 00:15:49.020
It can completely change

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:51.065
and you can try new things at any age.

00:15:51.065 --> 00:15:54.420
And I think with more
wisdom and more desire

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:55.770
and appreciation.

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:57.900
- I think women get braver
when they get older.

00:15:57.900 --> 00:15:59.160
- Absolutely.
- Absolutely.

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:01.800
And 'cause you know, what
the hell do we have to lose?

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:03.486
- Yeah.
- Why not?

00:16:03.486 --> 00:16:04.440
(audience applauds)

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:06.787
It's like the one thing, you
know, when people ask you like,

00:16:06.787 --> 00:16:08.940
"What would you tell your younger self?"

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:10.257
It's like, be braver.

00:16:10.257 --> 00:16:13.140
Don't listen to the nonsense, the chaos

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:15.120
that says what you should be saying

00:16:15.120 --> 00:16:17.540
or how you should be
acting or all those things.

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:19.878
Do you agree about like just growing older

00:16:19.878 --> 00:16:23.160
and you can become more
vibrant and more interesting?

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:24.690
I think that's the case.

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:26.400
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:29.460
When I was a kid, I
used to go into a party.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:33.900
I was traumatized by just the
experience of a lot of people.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:34.800
And I would, ugh.

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:39.800
But now I'm just, I have
a barn in Connecticut, I--

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:42.960
- Did you say a bar?

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:43.793
- Barn?

00:16:43.793 --> 00:16:47.100
- I'm more interested in the bar.

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:48.510
- You should have a bar in your barn.

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:49.343
- [Diane] She should.

00:16:49.343 --> 00:16:52.170
- I have a bar my barn. (chuckles)

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:53.040
There's no animals.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:53.940
It's a party barn.

00:16:54.930 --> 00:16:56.280
Diane, you'd love it.

00:16:56.280 --> 00:16:57.330
- Yeah, count me in.

00:16:57.330 --> 00:16:58.786
I mean, what the heck.

00:16:58.786 --> 00:17:02.040
- So Diane, how have you been
able to avoid diminishing

00:17:02.040 --> 00:17:02.873
in your own lives?

00:17:02.873 --> 00:17:03.706
I'll start with you.

00:17:03.706 --> 00:17:04.860
How do you think you've avoided that?

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:07.740
- I've always had issues with diminishing.

00:17:07.740 --> 00:17:08.573
- Really?

00:17:08.573 --> 00:17:10.440
- I like to, yeah, I like to head out.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:11.874
- It's so funny how we see ourselves.

00:17:11.874 --> 00:17:12.707
- I'm afraid of people,
like a lot of them.

00:17:12.707 --> 00:17:15.270
- She's compulsively creative.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:16.398
- Yeah.
- She is.

00:17:16.398 --> 00:17:18.990
- You think?
- She is constantly creative.

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:20.550
Constantly.

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:21.717
Watching her on the set,

00:17:21.717 --> 00:17:24.510
because we were on these amazing sets

00:17:24.510 --> 00:17:27.360
and when you couldn't find
her she was drifting off

00:17:27.360 --> 00:17:28.830
taking photographs of some--

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:31.350
- Sorry, I didn't, swear
to God, I was working hard.

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:32.820
- Yeah, she worked hard.
(audience laughs)

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:35.730
- Because the part is--
- The director is right there.

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:39.051
- The director and the
producer right there.

00:17:39.051 --> 00:17:42.493
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:17:42.493 --> 00:17:43.890
- I loved it.

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:46.380
How she's describing
herself as kind of like,

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:47.730
you know when you see that kid,

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:51.330
the six year old playing baseball
and he is just out there.

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:52.920
- [Mary] Yeah. That's her.

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:53.753
- That's me.

00:17:53.753 --> 00:17:54.930
Thanks for nothing.

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:55.980
But it's true.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:57.390
She's right.
- I love that though.

00:17:57.390 --> 00:17:59.910
I think that that keeps you
young, constantly being creative

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:02.220
and constantly excited about a project.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:03.660
I've always got so many projects going on,

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:05.460
not even in the limelight, just in life,

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:06.690
I like doing things.

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:09.420
I think that keeps you interesting

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:10.680
and excited about life.

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:12.515
- And engaged.
- And productive.

00:18:12.515 --> 00:18:13.348
- Yeah.

00:18:13.348 --> 00:18:14.430
- And also it's so amazing

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:16.320
what you've done with your life.

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:19.080
Because the first time I
saw you was on that show.

00:18:19.080 --> 00:18:19.913
- Oh American Idol, yeah, yeah.

00:18:19.913 --> 00:18:21.300
- Yes. I mean, my God.

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:22.440
And it's just--
- 20 years ago.

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:23.820
- It's so interesting.

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:26.850
- Yeah. And look, I'm
more shocked than anyone

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:28.440
that it would work out.
(everyone laughs)

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:31.456
I can promise you.
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:18:31.456 --> 00:18:32.733
I'm still shocked.

